- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a compact prefix-search data structure and implement tie-breaking logic using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequencies stored at each node
- Prefix search via traversal to the prefix end node
- DFS collection of all words in the subtree
- Sorting by frequency (descending), then lexicographic order for ties

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Autocomplete {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  addWord(word, freq) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.freq = freq;
  }

  getSuggestions(prefix, limit) {
    if (limit <= 0) return [];

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (currentNode, path) => {
      if (currentNode.freq > 0) {
        results.push({ word: path, freq: currentNode.freq });
      }
      for (const [char, childNode] of currentNode.children) {
        dfs(childNode, path + char);
      }
    };

    dfs(node, prefix);

    results.sort((a, b) => {
      if (b.freq !== a.freq) return b.freq - a.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });

    return results.slice(0, limit).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

